.Dd August 20, 2026
.Dt CAIRN 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm cairn
.Nd control and query a cairnd daemon
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl s Ar path | Fl a Ar addr
.Op Fl t Ar token
.Op Fl -timeout Ar secs
.Op Fl -json
.Ar command
.Op Ar arg ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
speaks the same HTTP API as any other client, over the local socket.
It has no privileged access and no side channel: anything it can do,
.Xr curl 1
can do.
.Pp
Metadata commands print a report meant to be read.
.Fl -json
prints the daemon's own answer instead, unchanged, and that is the form a
script should read: the reports are for people and their layout is not an
interface.
.Cm get
writes entry bytes and
.Cm head
writes response headers, in both modes.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width "-a addr"
.It Fl s , Fl -socket Ar path
Unix socket.
Default
.Pa /run/cairn/cairn.sock .
.It Fl a , Fl -address Ar addr
Loopback TCP address instead of a socket.
.It Fl t , Fl -token Ar token
Bearer token, when the daemon requires one.
.It Fl -timeout Ar secs
Read and write timeout.
Default 30.
.It Fl -json
Print the daemon's JSON rather than a report.
Accepted before or after the command.
.It Fl V , Fl -version
Print the version and exit.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print usage and exit.
.El
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm status
Daemon state, and the confinement actually applied.
Read this before trusting a deployment: it is where you learn that Landlock or
seccomp did not take.
.It Cm archives
Open archives, with their uuids.
The uuid is the id used everywhere else.
.Ql SUGGEST
says whether
.Cm suggest
can answer for that archive at all.
.It Cm archive Ar uuid
One archive, its
.Ql M
namespace metadata, and the names of any metadata that is not text.
.It Cm get Ar uuid path
Entry content, to standard output.
Redirects are followed by the daemon.
.It Cm head Ar uuid path
Response headers only, including the resolved path.
.It Cm suggest Ar uuid query Op Ar limit
Title-prefix suggestions.
Matching is byte-exact on the stored title.
An archive with no title ordering says so rather than answering with an empty
list that looks like a miss.
.It Cm random Ar uuid
One random entry path, alone on a line, so it can be fed straight back to
.Cm get .
.It Cm raw Ar method target
Any request target, for debugging.
The body is printed unchanged.
.El
.Sh ENTRY CONTENT
.Cm get
is a byte pipe.
Redirected or piped, standard output is the bytes as stored, exactly.
.Pp
Written to a terminal it is not, because a terminal is an interpreter and an
archive is hostile input: an entry read at a prompt has the characters a
terminal would act on replaced with
.Ql \&. ,
keeping its own newlines and tabs, and content that is not text is refused
rather than left to wedge the terminal.
Redirect it or pipe it to get the bytes.
.Sh ARCHIVE TEXT
Titles, paths, MIME types and metadata values are all chosen by whoever built
the archive.
Everything
.Nm
prints in a report has the control characters and bidirectional overrides
replaced with
.Ql \&. ,
so an archive cannot clear the screen, retitle the window, or forge a row in a
table.
The JSON is left alone:
.Xr cairnd 8
escapes control characters on the wire, so
.Fl -json
reports what an archive actually says.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
The request succeeded.
.It 1
The daemon answered with an error status, or could not be reached.
The failure is printed to standard error; with
.Fl -json
the error document is printed to standard output instead.
.It 2
Usage error.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Find an archive, then read something out of it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ cairn archives
UUID                                  ENTRIES  CLUSTERS  SUGGEST  TITLE
b10db2a4-0aac-52db-fd17-c5f79f36ab96    20317       389  yes      Climate Change

$ cairn suggest b10db2a4-0aac-52db-fd17-c5f79f36ab96 Clim
TITLE                  PATH
Climate change         Climate_change
Climate change denial  Climate_change_denial

$ cairn get b10db2a4-0aac-52db-fd17-c5f79f36ab96 Climate_change > article.html
.Ed
.Pp
A random article, fetched in one line:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ uuid=b10db2a4-0aac-52db-fd17-c5f79f36ab96
$ cairn get "$uuid" "$(cairn random "$uuid")" > article.html
.Ed
.Pp
For scripts, and against a daemon on loopback with a token:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ cairn --json status | jq .sandbox.layers
$ cairn --json archives | jq -r '.archives[] | "\e(.uuid) \e(.title)"'
$ cairn -a 127.0.0.1:8320 -t "$(cat /etc/cairn/token)" status
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cairn.conf 5 ,
.Xr cairn-api 7 ,
.Xr cairnd 8
